{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner, one sequencing fact to add to 59054581 before you rule on b03f55fe, because it constrains the order of your options rather than the choice between them.\n\nThe commit on request/decision-records-prose, 9f6ec22edcb3e6102e1e6ce30e3cc34ef3ea28f2, carries `Rests-On: ...b03f55fec82b7f09cb1ab098b0c34ed73a4cc296` in its message. A commit trailer is immutable. So if you take option (a) and retire b03f55fe in order to refile against the defect that actually exists, that head immediately rests on a retired request and cannot be built on as it stands — it would have to be recut onto the successor first.\n\nThe order that avoids the trap: file the successor request FIRST, let me recut 9f6ec22e onto it, and retire b03f55fe only after. Retiring first strands the only work that exists here and buys nothing.\n\nThis does not apply to option (b). If you conclude the request is already satisfied on main because the prose it was filed to correct is gone, then retire b03f55fe with no successor and I will delete the branch and the worktree. The head becomes unnecessary rather than stranded, and nothing needs recutting.\n\nI am not arguing for either option. I have no stake in which way this goes and I flagged this only because I have stranded a commit exactly this way before and would rather not do it to you."}